---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Tool from '../components/Tool.astro';
import BeforeAfter from '../components/BeforeAfter.astro';
import Decisions from '../components/Decisions.astro';
import ba from '../data/ba.json';
import { formatBytes } from '../lib/image/target-size';
import { SITE, SITE_NAME, LAST_UPDATED, abs } from '../config';
const ex = ba.after['20'], exBefore = formatBytes(ba.before.bytes), exAfter = formatBytes(ex.bytes), exPct = Math.round((ex.width / ba.before.width) * 100);
const jsonld = [
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebSite', name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE + '/' },
  { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'WebApplication', name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE + '/', applicationCategory: 'MultimediaApplication', operatingSystem: 'Any (modern web browser)', description: 'Free browser-based tool to compress image to 20KB or another target size in KB.' },
];
---
<Base title="Compress Image to 20KB Online – Free Image Compressor" description="Compress image to 20KB online for free with ImageTo20KB. Choose a target size, preview the result and download your smaller JPG, PNG or WebP in your browser." path="/" jsonld={jsonld}>
  <link slot="head" rel="preload" as="image" imagesrcset="/img/before-640.jpg 640w, /img/before-960.jpg 960w, /img/before.jpg 1200w" imagesizes="(max-width: 1100px) 46vw, 520px" media="(min-width: 821px)" fetchpriority="high" />
  <section class="hero hero-home">
    <div class="hero-bg" aria-hidden="true"><span class="blob b1"></span><span class="blob b2"></span><span class="blob b3"></span></div>
    <div class="wrap hero-grid">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Free · Private · No upload</p>
        <h1>Compress Image to 20KB Online Free</h1>
        <p class="lead lead-def">ImageTo20KB is a free online image compressor that helps students, job applicants and anyone filling in online forms compress image to 20KB. Add a JPG, PNG or WebP image, keep the 20KB target (or choose another size), and the tool compresses it in your browser and shows the real file size.</p>
        <p class="lead">Reduce JPG, JPEG, PNG, and supported image files to a smaller file size with ImageTo20KB. Choose a target size, preview the result, and download your compressed image. Your images are processed locally in your browser.</p>
        <div class="typed-line" aria-hidden="true">Perfect for <span class="typed" id="typed">online forms</span><span class="caret"></span></div>
        <div class="cta"><a class="btn" href="#tool" data-t="20">Compress to 20KB</a><a class="btn alt" href="#tool" data-t="100">Compress to 100KB</a></div>
        <ul class="chips" aria-label="Highlights"><li>Free</li><li>Runs in your browser</li><li>JPG, PNG and WebP</li><li>Batch and ZIP</li></ul>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-ba"><BeforeAfter target={20} variant="hero" /></div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <Tool target={20} heading="Compress image to 20KB: free online tool" />
    <BeforeAfter target={20} class="ba-mobile-only" />
    <article class="prose">
      <h2>How to compress image to 20KB online in four steps</h2>
      <ol class="steps">
        <li><strong>Choose your image.</strong> Drag a JPG, PNG or WebP file onto the tool, or click it to browse. You can add several files at once.</li>
        <li><strong>Keep the 20KB target.</strong> The 20KB preset is already selected. For a different limit, pick another preset or type a custom size.</li>
        <li><strong>Check the result.</strong> The result card shows the original and final size, the dimensions and a before-and-after preview, and says plainly whether the target was reached.</li>
        <li><strong>Download.</strong> Save the file, or use the ZIP button for several images. Everything happens in your browser, so nothing is uploaded.</li>
      </ol>
      <h2>Why forms ask you to compress image to 20KB</h2>
      <p>Online application forms set small upload limits so that thousands of files can be stored and loaded quickly. Signature fields are the most common case: notices from exam bodies such as SSC are commonly reported with a signature range of about 10 to 20 KB. Job portals and scholarship pages use similar small limits for passport-style photos. If your form says "maximum 20KB", you need to compress image to 20KB before the upload is accepted.</p>
      <p>Check the exact range on your own form, because limits differ by exam and year. The <a href="/government-exam-photo-signature-size/">government exam photo and signature guide</a> lists commonly reported ranges and explains how to handle a minimum size. If 20KB is too small for your form, you can compress image to 100KB online instead.</p>
      <h2>A real example: {exBefore} to {exAfter}</h2>
      <p>The slider on this page shows a real result from the tool. The original is a {ba.before.width}×{ba.before.height} pixel JPEG of {exBefore}. For this image compress to 20KB job, the tool searched for the highest JPEG quality that fits 20,480 bytes. It settled on {ex.quality}% quality at {ex.width}×{ex.height} pixels ({exPct}% of the original width), producing a {exAfter} file.</p>
      <p>The same method applies to every image: measure the real file size, keep quality reasonable, and shrink dimensions only as much as needed. If an image cannot reach the target, the tool says so and shows the smallest result it could make.</p>
      <h2>Which target size fits your need?</h2>
      <table class="size-table">
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Target</th><th scope="col">Typical use</th><th scope="col">What to expect</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">20KB</th><td>Signatures, small passport photos, strict upload fields</td><td>A very small picture, often a few hundred pixels wide, with some softness on detailed photos</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">50KB</th><td>Form photos and signatures with a 50KB limit</td><td>A clearer face than 20KB, still at small dimensions</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">100KB</th><td>Typical application and portal uploads</td><td>Good clarity at roughly 800 to 1,200 pixels wide</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">200KB</th><td>Web uploads, documents, email</td><td>Sharp on most screens</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">500KB</th><td>High-quality sharing and scans</td><td>Close to the original look for most photos</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="note">These are typical results, not guarantees: they depend on your image and the format you choose.</p>
      <h2>Tips to compress image to 20KB with a cleaner result</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>Start from the original photo, not a copy sent through a messaging app.</li>
        <li>Crop away what you do not need, so the 20,480 bytes are spent on the face or signature.</li>
        <li>Choose JPEG, and set a maximum width of about 400 to 600 pixels for photos.</li>
        <li>Avoid compressing the same file repeatedly, because each lossy save removes detail.</li>
        <li>Aim for about 19KB if a portal might count 1KB as 1,000 bytes.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These habits help whenever you need an image compress to 20KB from a large photo, and they keep the result sharper than lowering quality alone.</p>
      <Decisions slug="/" />
      <h2>Compress image to 20KB with a target-size tool, not a slider</h2>
      <p>Most image compressors, including the open-source Squoosh image compression app from Google Chrome Labs, give you sliders and let you tune codecs by hand. That is powerful for exploring, but slow when a form simply says maximum 20KB. ImageTo20KB works the other way round: you enter the size, it searches for the best fit, and it reports the real result. Each approach suits a different job, and we are not affiliated with Squoosh.</p>
      <p>For a quick image compress to 20KB request, a target-size tool saves guesswork. For fine control over codecs and formats, a manual tool is a better choice.</p>
      <h2>Key facts about ImageTo20KB</h2>
      <ul class="facts">
        <li>Input formats: JPG/JPEG, PNG and WebP. Not supported: animated GIF, animated WebP, SVG and HEIC.</li>
        <li>Output formats: JPEG, WebP or PNG, depending on what your browser can encode.</li>
        <li>Targets: presets of 20, 50, 100, 200 and 500 KB, or any custom size from 1 to 10,240 KB.</li>
        <li>Unit: 1 KB is counted as 1,024 bytes, so 20KB is 20,480 bytes and 100KB is 102,400 bytes.</li>
        <li>Limits: 50 MB and 50 megapixels per file, to protect your browser from running out of memory.</li>
        <li>Privacy: compression runs in your browser; images are not uploaded for compression.</li>
        <li>Batch: several images at once, with a ZIP download of the successful results.</li>
        <li>Maintained by Kamlesh Mohabe, Software Engineer. Last reviewed {LAST_UPDATED}.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>Explore related tools and guides</h2>
      <ul class="links">
        <li><a href="/compress-image-to-20kb/">Compress image to 20KB online</a></li>
        <li><a href="/blog/how-to-compress-image-to-20kb/">Guide: how to compress image to 20KB</a></li>
        <li><a href="/compress-image-to-100kb/">Compress image to 100KB</a></li>
        <li><a href="/image-compressor/">General image compressor</a></li>
        <li><a href="/image-resizer/">Image resizer</a></li>
        <li><a href="/government-exam-photo-signature-size/">Government exam photo and signature size</a></li>
        <li><a href="/blog/">Blog: photo and signature upload help</a></li>
        <li><a href="/faqs/">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
        <li><a href="/how-it-works/">How the compression works</a></li>
      </ul>
    </article>
  </div>
  <script>
    const el = document.getElementById('typed');
    const phrases = ['online forms', 'SSC and RRB photos', 'UPSC signatures', 'job applications', 'email attachments', '20KB, 50KB or 100KB targets'];
    if (el && !matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches) {
      let i = 0, n = el.textContent!.length, del = true;
      const tick = () => {
        const full = phrases[i];
        if (!del) { n++; el.textContent = full.slice(0, n); if (n === full.length) { del = true; return void setTimeout(tick, 1700); } return void setTimeout(tick, 65); }
        n--; el.textContent = phrases[i].slice(0, Math.max(n, 0));
        if (n <= 0) { del = false; i = (i + 1) % phrases.length; n = 0; return void setTimeout(tick, 350); }
        setTimeout(tick, 32);
      };
      setTimeout(tick, 2500);
    }
    document.querySelectorAll<HTMLAnchorElement>('[data-t]').forEach((a) => a.addEventListener('click', () => {
      document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>(`[data-preset="${a.dataset.t}"]`)?.click();
    }));
  </script>
</Base>
